        <div id="why-different" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why a Zipline Harness Is Different
          </h2>

          <p>
            The instinct is to reach for a climbing harness, and that instinct is only partly right. Climbing and ziplining load a harness in different ways, and the differences explain most of what separates a good zipline harness from an adequate one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first difference is <strong>sustained suspension</strong>. A climber hangs in a harness in short bursts — working a move, resting on a rope, being lowered. A zipline rider hangs continuously through the ride and often while waiting at a platform, and on a long course that adds up. Sustained hanging concentrates pressure at the top of the thighs, and if the harness does not spread that load across a wide area it restricts circulation. Narrow leg loops that are perfectly fine for climbing become genuinely uncomfortable, and in extended suspension, harness-induced suspension trauma is a recognised medical risk rather than a theoretical one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is <strong>posture and attachment height</strong>. Where the harness attaches to the trolley determines how you hang. A low, ventral attachment point holds a comfortable seated position. A higher attachment point holds you more upright but changes the balance, and an attachment that is too low relative to your centre of mass lets you tip backward. Purpose-built suspension harnesses position their attachment points deliberately; general-purpose harnesses do not always.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is <strong>the possibility of inverting</strong>. A sit harness stays on because your hips hold it there. Turn upside down and that assumption fails. On a zipline, inversion can come from an awkward launch, a spin, or a brake that stops the trolley faster than it stops the body. This is why full-body harnesses exist and why they are not optional for children.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
