        <!-- SECTION 2 -->
        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Certifications That Actually Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the section to read if you read nothing else. A certification mark tells you what load case a harness was tested against, and a harness with no certification at all tells you nothing whatsoever about whether it will hold.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">EN 813 — Sit Harnesses for Suspension</h3>
          <p>
            The standard written for harnesses intended to hold a person in a seated position for sustained periods. It is the most directly relevant rating for ziplining, and it is the one to look for on an adult sit harness that will see repeated or long rides. Harnesses carrying it are designed with wide, load-spreading waistbelts and leg loops and a low ventral attachment point.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">EN 361 — Full-Body Fall Arrest</h3>
          <p>
            Covers full-body harnesses designed to catch a falling person safely and keep them upright afterward. Relevant wherever inversion is possible, and the rating that matters on an adult full-body harness. The American equivalent is ANSI Z359, which covers full-body fall protection harnesses to a broadly comparable intent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">EN 12277 — Climbing Harnesses</h3>
          <p>
            The climbing standard, subdivided by type. Type C is an adult sit harness; Type B is specifically for children under 30 kg; Type A is a full-body adult harness. A Type C climbing harness is usable for short adult zipline rides and is what many commercial operations issue, but it has not been assessed for prolonged suspension the way EN 813 has.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">EN 358 — Work Positioning</h3>
          <p>
            Covers harnesses and lanyards used to hold a worker in position at a work site. It appears alongside other ratings on professional harnesses and is not on its own sufficient for suspension or fall arrest.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Never Ride on an Uncertified or Unknown Harness</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              A harness with no manufacturer markings, no certification mark, and no date of manufacture cannot be assessed and should not be used. The same applies to a secondhand harness whose history you cannot verify — a harness that has arrested a hard fall, been stored against chemicals, or spent years in UV can be structurally compromised with no visible sign. Improvised webbing, rope tied into a seat, or a repurposed dog harness are not substitutes and have killed people. This is one of the few pieces of outdoor equipment where buying used is genuinely a bad idea.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

