        <div id="sit-vs-full-body" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sit vs Full-Body Harnesses
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the decision that determines which half of this guide applies to you, and it is not primarily about comfort.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sit Harnesses</h3>
          <p>
            A sit harness consists of a waistbelt and two leg loops, and it stays on because your hips are wider than your waist. It hangs comfortably, it is quick to fit, and for an upright adult on a short ride it is entirely appropriate — it is what most commercial courses issue. Its limitation is that it depends on remaining upright. Invert, and the only thing holding you in is friction and your own grip.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Full-Body Harnesses</h3>
          <p>
            A full-body harness adds shoulder straps and a chest or sternal attachment point, so the wearer cannot come out of it in any orientation and is held upright automatically. This is mandatory for children, strongly advisable where a rider might invert, and the standard configuration on steeper or higher courses. The cost is comfort — most full-body harnesses are fall-arrest designs and hang less pleasantly than a dedicated sit harness — and fitting complexity.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Combination Systems</h3>
          <p>
            A sit harness paired with a chest harness gets both: the suspension comfort of a purpose-built seat and the anti-inversion security of a full body. This is what professional guides wear. It costs the most, takes the longest to fit, and is genuinely the best answer for anyone riding frequently.
          </p>

