        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <div id="fit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Fit, Sizing and Suspension Comfort
          </h2>

          <p>
            A correctly certified harness fitted badly is not a safe harness. Fit is where most real-world problems live, and it is the part that gets rushed when a group is waiting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The waistbelt goes above the hip bones</strong>, not around the waist in the fashion sense and not on the hips. It should be snug enough that you cannot pull it down over the iliac crest — that bony ridge is the thing physically stopping the harness from coming off. Two fingers of slack is the usual guidance; if you can fit a flat hand under it, it is too loose.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Leg loops should be snug but not tight</strong>, with roughly a flat hand's width of movement. Too loose and you shift within the harness under load, concentrating pressure unpredictably; too tight and you restrict circulation before you even leave the platform.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Every buckle must be correctly threaded and doubled back</strong> unless it is an automatic buckle designed otherwise. Check the manufacturer's instructions for the specific model — threading conventions differ, and a buckle that looks closed but is not doubled back can slip under load. This is the single most common fitting error.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check for twisted webbing</strong> anywhere in the system before clipping in, especially on full-body harnesses where there is simply more webbing to twist. A twisted strap loads unevenly and can concentrate force on a narrow edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, a note on <strong>suspension comfort as a safety issue</strong> rather than a luxury. Prolonged motionless suspension in a harness restricts venous return from the legs and can, in extended cases, cause a person to lose consciousness — a recognised phenomenon usually called suspension trauma or orthostatic intolerance. It is not a concern for a fifteen-second ride. It becomes one if a rider stalls mid-line and hangs motionless for a long period, which is exactly why rescue plans exist on commercial courses and why anyone building their own system needs a way to get a stalled rider down promptly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6 -->
        <div id="backyard" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Backyard Ziplines: A Reality Check
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people searching for a zipline harness are not buying for a commercial course — they are building or using a home zipline. It is worth being direct about where the risk actually sits, because it is mostly not in the harness.
          </p>

          <p>
            The harness is one component of a system. The others are the cable and its tension, the anchors and whatever they are attached to, the trolley, the braking system, the connectors and lanyard, and the ground clearance along the entire span. In the incident reports that exist, injuries on home ziplines overwhelmingly involve <strong>braking, ground clearance, and anchor failure</strong> rather than harness failure. Riders hit the end stop too fast, or their feet strike the ground at speed on a sagging cable, or a tree anchor fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cable sag is the specific thing people get wrong. A cable tensioned too tightly puts enormous loads on the anchors — far higher than the rider's weight — and can fail them. A cable too loose sags in the middle and drops the rider toward the ground at the fastest point of the ride. Getting that balance right is an engineering calculation, not a judgment call, and it changes with span, rider weight, and temperature.
          </p>

          <p>
            Trees are living anchors. They move, they grow, they rot from the inside, and hardware installed through them changes over years. A tree that was a sound anchor when the line was installed may not be one three seasons later.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical advice: if you are installing a home zipline, use a complete kit from a reputable manufacturer, install it strictly to the instructions, respect the stated span and weight limits rather than treating them as guidance, and have the installation inspected by someone qualified — an arborist for the trees, and a professional familiar with challenge-course or rope-access standards for the system. Inspect the whole system before every session, not just the harness. And have a plan for getting a stalled rider down, because that situation is far more likely than a catastrophic failure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="inspection" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Inspection, Care and Retirement
          </h2>

          <p>
            Textile safety equipment has a finite life, and unlike a rope or a carabiner, a harness rarely gives obvious warning before it is compromised.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Inspect before every use.</strong> Run the webbing through your hands looking for cuts, abrasion, fuzzing, or stiffness. Check every stitch line, particularly at the attachment points and where leg loops join the waistbelt — broken or pulled stitching is the clearest single indicator to retire a harness. Check buckles for cracks, deformation, or grit, and check that adjustment still holds under a firm pull.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store it dry, dark and clean.</strong> UV degrades nylon and polyester steadily, and a harness left hanging in sunlight loses strength invisibly. Chemicals are worse — acids in particular attack nylon severely, and a harness stored next to a car battery or solvents may be compromised with no visible sign at all. Wash with clean water and mild soap only, air dry away from direct heat, and never machine dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Respect the service life.</strong> Manufacturers typically specify a maximum of ten years from the date of manufacture for textile products, with a much shorter working life under frequent use — often three to five years, and less in commercial service. Check the manufacturer's own instructions rather than a general rule, and record the date of first use somewhere you will find it again.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Retire immediately, regardless of age</strong>, if the harness has arrested a significant fall, shows any cut or serious abrasion, has damaged stitching, has been exposed to chemicals, has been in contact with sustained high heat, or has any history you cannot personally verify. When a harness is retired, cut it up before disposing of it so nobody retrieves it and uses it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage location matters more than people assume. A garage is the usual home for outdoor gear and an unsealed concrete slab wicks moisture continuously — our <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">garage floor epoxy guide</a> covers coatings that stop it, and a <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">dehumidifier sized for the space</a> handles humid climates. Store harnesses hanging in a dry, dark cupboard rather than in a damp bin on the floor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: FAQ -->
