            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best harness for zipline riding?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For adults riding repeatedly, the Petzl AVAO SIT FAST is the best overall choice because it carries EN 813 certification for sustained suspension and distributes load across a wide waistbelt and leg loops. For maximum hanging comfort the Petzl FALCON is better still. For children under 30 kg, a full-body harness such as the Petzl SIMBA or Edelrid Fraggle III is the correct and safest option.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a climbing harness for ziplining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A climbing harness certified to EN 12277 can be used for short zipline rides by adults, and many commercial courses do exactly that. It is not ideal for sustained suspension: EN 12277 covers climbing falls and short hangs, while EN 813 is the standard written for prolonged seated suspension. On longer rides a climbing harness concentrates pressure in the leg loops and becomes uncomfortable much faster. Never use an uncertified harness, improvised webbing, or a harness of unknown history.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do children need a full-body harness for a zipline?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A young child's hips are proportionally narrow relative to their waist and torso, so a sit harness can slide upward and off under load or if the child inverts. A full-body harness certified to EN 12277 Type B for children under 30 kg has shoulder straps and a high chest attachment point that make this impossible and keep the child upright. This is not an area to improvise or size up an adult harness.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What certification should a zipline harness have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                EN 813 is the European standard for sit harnesses intended for sustained suspension and is the most relevant single rating for ziplining. EN 361 covers full-body fall-arrest harnesses and matters where inversion is possible. EN 12277 covers climbing harnesses, with Type B specifically for children under 30 kg. In the United States, ANSI Z359 covers full-body fall protection harnesses. Any harness used on a zipline should carry at least one relevant certification, clear manufacturer markings, and a traceable date of manufacture.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a zipline harness last before it must be retired?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most manufacturers specify a maximum service life of ten years from the date of manufacture for textile harnesses, and a much shorter working life under frequent use — often three to five years, or less in commercial service. A harness must be retired immediately regardless of age if it has arrested a significant fall, shows cut or abraded webbing, has damaged stitching, has been exposed to chemicals, or has any history you cannot verify. Follow the manufacturer's instructions, not a general rule.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a harness enough to make a backyard zipline safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The harness is one component in a system that also includes the cable, its tension and sag, the anchors and the trees or structures they are attached to, the trolley, the braking system, the lanyard and connectors, and the ground clearance along the whole span. Most serious backyard zipline injuries involve braking, ground clearance, or anchor failure rather than the harness. A home zipline should be designed and inspected by a qualified professional, and consumer kits should be installed strictly to their instructions with the stated weight and span limits respected.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes When Buying and Using a Zipline Harness
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every one of these is common, and every one of them is a decision made before anybody leaves the platform.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Child in an Adult Sit Harness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tightening an adult sit harness down onto a small child does not make it fit — a child's hips cannot retain it, and it can slide off entirely under load or inversion. Children under 30 kg need a full-body harness certified to EN 12277 Type B. There is no acceptable improvisation here, and this is the most dangerous mistake in this guide.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an Uncertified or Secondhand Harness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Marketplace listings for cheap unbranded harnesses with no certification mark are common, and so are used harnesses sold with no history. A harness that has taken a hard fall or been stored badly can be compromised with no visible sign. Buy new, from a manufacturer that prints its certifications and date of manufacture on the product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rushing the Buckle Check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A buckle that is threaded but not doubled back looks closed and can slip under load. This is the most common fitting error, and it happens when someone is moving a queue along. Build a fixed habit: waistbelt above the hip bones, every buckle checked and doubled back, no twisted webbing, then clip in. Check your own, then have someone else check it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the Harness as the Whole Safety System</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A perfect harness on a badly tensioned cable with no braking plan protects nobody. On home installations, the cable, anchors, trolley, brake, and ground clearance deserve far more scrutiny than the harness does — that is where the serious injuries actually come from. Get the installation professionally inspected and inspect the whole system before every session.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

